  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1228 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nA complex of storms is progged to develop over the High Plains\nTuesday night with the remnants dropping southeast into central\nOklahoma by Wednesday morning. This will likely lead to the\ndevelopment of an MCV that then track eastward across eastern\nOklahoma through the afternoon Wednesday. Hot and humid conditions\nahead of the MCV will lead to modest instability across mush of\nthe area by mid afternoon Wednesday. At least widely scattered to\nscattered thunderstorm development is expected along the leading\nedge of the MCV across eastern Oklahoma and into western Arkansas\nduring the afternoon hours. Higher effective shear, in \nassociation with the passing shortwave, and instability could lead\nto a few strong to severe storms, especially across far eastern \nOklahoma and western Arkansas, with damaging wind gusts the \nprimary concern. An effective cold front will also work southward\nthrough the day, with less hot conditions again expected across\nthe north Wednesday afternoon compared to locations south of I-40. \n\nAny thunderstorm activity could linger through the evening hours\nbefore departing to the southeast with the exiting shortwave.\nTemperatures nearer to normal will follow in the wake of the front\nfor Thursday and likely into Friday, though dry weather will also\npersist under surface ridging. Southerly flow returns for the\nweekend into early next week, while the mid level ridge builds\nback overhead. Therefore, temperatures will warm back into the\nupper 90s and 100s across the region to finish out the forecast\nperiod. Rain chances will be minimal through the period after\nWednesday.\n\nBowlan",
